<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alvor Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angolan-war-of-independence">Angolan War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alvor Agreement was signed on 15 January 1975 at Alvor in Portugal by the Portuguese government and the three Angolan liberation movements, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a>. It set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>'s independence for 11 November 1975, defined Cabinda as part of Angola, and created a transitional government shared by the movements. Fighting among them soon destroyed it, opening the Angolan civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alvor-agreement.jpg" alt="Alvor Agreement" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Portuguese government, FNLA, MPLA, UNITA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alvor_Agreement.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Leftist military officers overthrew the Caetano government in Portugal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 25 April 1974. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> each negotiated with the new Portuguese authorities while fighting one another for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> and the rest of the country. Their leaders, Holden Roberto, Agostinho Neto and Jonas Savimbi, met at Bukavu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> in July and agreed to negotiate as one body, then at Mombasa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> on 5 January 1975, where they agreed to stop fighting and outlined a joint position. Talks at Alvor followed from 10 to 15 January.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Elections to a National Assembly were set for October 1975. Until independence a transitional government would rule, made up of the Portuguese High Commissioner, Admiral Rosa Coutinho, and a Prime Ministerial Council of one representative from each Angolan party, with a rotating premiership and decisions needing two-thirds support. The twelve ministries were split three each among the three parties and Portugal. Only the three movements could nominate candidates, which excluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flec">Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda</a>, the Cabindese and white Angolans from standing on their own.</p><p>The fighters of the three movements were to join 24,000 Black soldiers of the Portuguese Army in a single force, the Angolan Defense Forces, of 48,000, with 8,000 from each party, though every military decision required unanimous consent. The author Witney Wright Schneidman argued that the division of power produced a virtual paralysis of executive authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>No mechanism checked the size of each force, and the movements soon outnumbered the Portuguese troops. Fighting resumed as foreign arms arrived, and a conference at Nakuru in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, moderated by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a>, produced only a declaration on 21 June. Many analysts have blamed the Portuguese for favouring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a>; Coutinho openly handed it former Portuguese arms. In July the MPLA drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-of-angola">FNLA</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, and by August it held 11 of the 15 provincial capitals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> sent troops into the south on 23 October. On 11 November Neto declared the People's Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, while the FNLA and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> set up a rival government at Huambo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alvor Agreement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 11 November 1975 Angola became a sovereign state under the <b>Alvor Agreement</b>, as the People's Republic of Angola, which re-annexed the short-lived Republic of Cabinda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Alvor Agreement</b> of January 1975 set independence for 11 November 1975 and called for a coalition of the MPLA, the FNLA and UNITA.</blockquote><a href="/state/angola">Angola</a></li><li><blockquote>Separatists of the Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda claimed independence, but the 1975 <b>Alvor Agreement</b> made Cabinda part of independent Angola, where it remains a province with a low-level separatist conflict.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-congo">Portuguese Congo</a></li></ul></section>
